      },
      "title": "The shared state",
      "description": "**New idea — graph state:** in LangGraph, every node reads one shared dictionary and returns *only the parts it changes*. LangGraph merges those changes in.\n\n**How it works:** `GraphState` lists every key. `total=False` means none are required. The router fills `routing`, the specialist fills `tool_calls`, and so on.\n\n**Why it matters:** nodes don't call each other. They only talk through this state, which keeps each one simple to test and reason about.\n\n**Watch out:** `Annotated[..., add]` on `messages` means new messages are **appended**, not replaced. Without it, each node would overwrite the conversation."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/state.py",
      "line": 39,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 17,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 39,
          "character": 33
        }
      },
      "title": "A custom merge rule",
      "description": "**New idea — reducer:** a function that decides how a node's update combines with the existing value.\n\n**How it works:** tool calls are matched by `id`. An update to an existing call replaces it, and new calls are added.\n\n**Why it matters:** the specialist adds a call with `result: None`, then the tool executor returns the same call **with** a result. Without this rule you'd get two copies.\n\n**Watch out:** calls without an `id` are dropped by this merge. The model always gives ids, but it's a hidden assumption."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/state.py",
      "line": 72,
