        }
      },
      "title": "Nodes",
      "description": "**How it works:** `StateGraph(GraphState)` creates a graph for that state. `add_node` registers the four steps by name, and `START → router` says where every run begins.\n\n**Why it matters:** this is the whole agent, in a handful of lines.\n\n**Watch out:** the verifier, synthesizer and tool retriever nodes exist in `nodes/`, but they're never added here (atlas A1 covers them)."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/builder.py",
      "line": 215,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 169,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 215,
          "character": 17
        }
      },
      "title": "Edges",
      "description": "**New idea — conditional edges:** after a node runs, a small function looks at the state and returns the name of the next node. The dictionary maps that name to a real node, or to `END`.\n\n**How it works:**\n- router → entity loader, or end\n- entity loader → specialist, or end\n- specialist → tool executor, entity loader, router, or end\n- tool executor → specialist, or end\n\n**Why it matters:** the specialist ⇄ tool executor loop is how the agent calls a tool, reads the result, maybe calls another, then answers.\n\n**Watch out:** the graph is only *built* here. It's compiled (with the checkpointer) inside `run_graph`."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/builder.py",
      "line": 54,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 32,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 54,
          "character": 17
        }
      },
      "title": "Where to after the specialist?",
      "description": "**How it works:** in order:\n1. a topic change → back to the router\n2. new things were created → back to the entity loader\n3. tool calls waiting for results → tool executor\n4. otherwise → done\n\n**Why it matters:** this one function decides when the agent stops.\n\n**Watch out:** the order matters: a topic change would win even if tool calls were waiting. In practice nothing sets the first two flags yet (step 4), so the choice is tool executor or done."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/builder.py",
      "line": 85,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 57,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 85,
          "character": 24
        }
      },
      "title": "Where to after a tool?",
      "description": "**How it works:** stop on an error, or on a *blocking* problem like an expired Google token. Otherwise go back to the specialist, which reads the result and decides what's next.\n\n**Why it matters:** blocking problems end the run right away instead of making the model retry something that can't work.\n\n**Watch out:** both remaining branches return `specialist`. The pending-tools check is there for safety but doesn't change the outcome."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/builder.py",
      "line": 117,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 88,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 117,
          "character": 24
        }
      },
      "title": "Stopping early",
      "description": "**How it works:** after the router, stop if there was an error or no service. After the entity loader, stop if `action_required` is blocking, for example if you asked about AdMob but haven't connected it.\n\n**Why it matters:** stopping before the specialist saves an expensive model call when the answer can only be *please connect your account*.\n\n**Watch out:** the user still sees a clear message, because the entity loader's `action_required` becomes an SSE event (tour 06)."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/builder.py",
      "line": 267,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 233,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 267,
          "character": 26
        }
      },
      "title": "A quick look before routing",
      "description": "**How it works:** one fast call to the API's internal list of providers, with a 5-second timeout, to learn which networks you've connected.\n\n**Why it matters:** the router can then prefer networks you actually have. *Show my revenue* means AdMob if that's all you've connected.\n\n**Watch out:** it only spots AdMob and Ad Manager here (`admob`, `gam`). Other networks are found later by the entity loader. Any error gives an empty list, and routing continues."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/builder.py",
      "line": 354,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 331,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 354,
          "character": 6
        }
      },
      "title": "The starting state and the config",
      "description": "**How it works:** the run starts with your question, your context, the stream id and your history. The *config* carries the thread id, the two queues (so nodes can stream text and events), and `recursion_limit: 25`.\n\n**Why it matters:** queues travel in the config, not the state, because the state is saved to the database and queues can't be saved.\n\n**Watch out:** the 25-step limit protects against endless specialist ⇄ tool loops. Hitting it raises an error, which reaches you as an `error` event."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/builder.py",
      "line": 379,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 361,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 379,
          "character": 11
        }
      },
      "title": "Compile and stream",
      "description": "**How it works:** open the Postgres checkpointer, make sure its tables exist (`setup()`), compile the graph with it, then `astream` the run. Two kinds of output come back:\n- `updates`: what each node changed\n- `custom`: progress events nodes send while working\n\n**Why it matters:** this is the loop that tour 06's `run_graph_and_emit` consumes.\n\n**Watch out:** the graph is rebuilt, `setup()` runs, and a new database connection opens on **every** message. It works, but it's extra work per request."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/builder.py",
      "line": 230,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 218,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 230,
          "character": 61
        }
      },
      "title": "The checkpointer",
      "description": "**New idea — checkpointer:** LangGraph saves the state after every node. If the run pauses, it can continue later from exactly that point.\n\n**How it works:** `AsyncPostgresSaver` stores those snapshots in your Neon Postgres database, keyed by thread id.\n\n**Why it matters:** this is what makes the approval pause work. The run stops, you click Allow, and it continues, even though that's a new HTTP request.\n\n**Watch out:** no `DATABASE_URL` means no runs at all, because this raises an error."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/builder.py",
      "line": 442,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 440,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 442,
          "character": 57
        }
      },
      "title": "Resuming after approval",
      "description": "**How it works:** `resume_graph` rebuilds and compiles the graph with the same checkpointer, then sends `Command(resume={approved, modified_params})` to the same thread. LangGraph reloads the saved state and continues inside the tool executor, right where `interrupt()` paused.\n\n**Why it matters:** human-in-the-loop with no thread waiting around. The paused run is just rows in Postgres.\n\n**Watch out:** the thread id is the 8-character stream id from tour 06. Lose it and the paused run can't be resumed. Tour 11 covers the full approval round-trip."
    },
    {
